avcodec/videotoolboxdec: a standalone H.264 decoder on VideoToolbox

goffmpeg #77. Upstream: not submitted yet.

FFmpeg decodes H.264 through VideoToolbox only as a hwaccel of its own
h264 decoder (h264_videotoolbox_hwaccel_select="h264_decoder"), which
parses every slice in software and hands VideoToolbox one picture at a
time. A build can't have VideoToolbox's H.264 without FFmpeg's.

h264_videotoolbox is a decoder of its own, as h264_mediacodec is on
Android. It takes the demuxer's packets whole, MP4's length-prefixed NAL
units or MPEG-TS's Annex B, keeps the parameter sets (parsed with
h264_ps, and their bytes for CMVideoFormatDescriptionCreateFromH264Parameter
Sets), and gives VideoToolbox each access unit as 4-byte length-prefixed
NAL units, synchronously. The pictures, which VideoToolbox returns in
decoding order, are put into presentation order by timestamp within an
IDR's generation, holding the SPS's num_reorder_frames, so a stream whose
timestamps jump back at an IDR (an HLS discontinuity, two streams joined)
comes out in decoding order across it. They are copied out as NV12 with
the SPS's size, cropping, colour description and aspect ratio, as h264
sets them.

A new SPS makes a new session, and the frames already decoded keep their
size. A session iOS invalidates (as when the app goes to the background)
is made again. A picture VideoToolbox can't decode is dropped and decoding
goes on. Only 8-bit 4:2:0 is given to VideoToolbox: High 4:4:4 and 10-bit
are refused at open.

Measured on macOS 27 (arm64), against FFmpeg's h264, as framemd5 of
yuv420p: every H.264 clip in goffmpeg's testdata (320x240 to 1080p, MP4,
MPEG-TS from HLS, a stream with B-frames), a 1080p30 and a 720x720 clip
(900 and 300 frames), clips tagged BT.709 and full range, and a stream
that changes from 320x240 to 640x360 with overlapping timestamps: the
same frames, timestamps and colour tags. The same after seeks to 0.5,
1.5 and 2.2 s. In a build with no h264 decoder, stream probing uses this
one, and the frames are the same. A clip with 3000 damaged bytes gives
the same frame count, 96 of 99 frames identical.

